A local frame does not arbitrarily select its Gam and Tick. Its physically available reference wave supplies them. The Zero frame owns its declared reference wave; the present Local frame is provisionally calibrated by the Local EM wave-formation interface scale $$\mathrm{Gam}_L=8.86\times10^{-22}\,\mathrm m,
\qquad
\mathrm{Tick}_L=\frac{\mathrm{Gam}_L}{c}
=2.955377883455627\times10^{-30}\,\mathrm s.$$ The common stretch factor is $$\boxed{D=\frac{\mathrm{Gam}_L}{\mathrm{Gam}_0}
=\frac{\mathrm{Tick}_L}{\mathrm{Tick}_0}
\approx1.740299661490488\times10^{12}.}$$

For equal length and time stretch, $$\boxed{M_L=\frac{M_0}{D}
\approx2.49460394391\times10^{-21}\,\mathrm{kg}},$$ $$\boxed{E_L=\frac{E_0}{D}
\approx2.24203821349\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm J.}$$ Physical electron mass is provisionally invariant, while its numerical coefficient changes with the unit: $$\mu_e^L=\frac{m_e}{M_L}=D\mu_e^0
\approx3.65163525703\times10^{-10}.$$ The local Compton wavelength and period therefore have reciprocal coefficients consistent with the standard local calibration.

For $$\nu_{C,e}=\frac{m_ec_0^2}{h},
\qquad
\lambda_{C,e}=\frac{h}{m_ec_0},$$ the unified dimensionless coefficient is $$\boxed{\mu_e=
\nu_{C,e}\mathrm{Tick}_0
=\frac{m_e}{M_0}
=\frac{E_e}{E_0}
=\frac{\mathrm{Gam}_0}{\lambda_{C,e}}
=2.0982795881827835\times10^{-22}.}$$ Its inverse is $$\boxed{\mu_e^{-1}=4.765809121109788\times10^{21}.}$$ Thus the electron completes one non-reduced Compton cycle in approximately $4.7658\times10^{21}$ zero-frame Ticks, and one zero-frame energy unit is equivalent to the rest energy of the same number of electrons.
